LHS = \dfrac{1}{1(1 + 1)} = \dfrac{1}{2}        RHS = \dfrac{1}{1 + 1} = \dfrac{1}{2}
∴ true for n=1
Step 2: Let n=k be a number for which the statement is true
i.e. \dfrac{1}{1 \times 2} + \dfrac{1}{2 \times 3} + \dfrac{1}{3 \times 4} + \dots + \dfrac{1}{k(k + 1)} = \dfrac{k}{k + 1}
Step 3: Prove true for n=k+1
i.e. \dfrac{1}{1 \times 2} + \dfrac{1}{2 \times 3} + \dfrac{1}{3 \times 4} + \dots + \dfrac{1}{k(k + 1)} + \dfrac{1}{(k + 1)(k + 2)} = \dfrac{k + 1}{k + 2}
LHS = \dfrac{1}{1 \times 2} + \dfrac{1}{2 \times 3} + \dfrac{1}{3 \times 4} + \dots + \dfrac{1}{k(k + 1)} + \dfrac{1}{(k + 1)(k + 2)}
= \dfrac{k}{k + 1} + \dfrac{1}{(k + 1)(k + 2)}        (from step 2)
= \dfrac{k(k + 2)}{(k + 1)(k + 2)} + \dfrac{1}{(k + 1)(k + 2)}
= \dfrac{k^{2} + 2k + 1}{(k + 1)(k + 2)}
= \dfrac{(k + 1)(k + 1)}{(k + 1)(k + 2)}
= \dfrac{k + 1}{k + 2} = RHS
∴ true for n=k+1
∴ by induction, the statement is true for all integers n ≥ 1

